From b1d6fc6efb513d278e47ed7eb57a56db64023c03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 06:21:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/11] tests: mdb_env_warm_dbis on an environment laid out as
 monerod's hash indexes

A generated environment with three tables, each with one zero key and
60,000 random fixed-size values as sorted duplicates, as monerod keeps
spent key images, transaction hashes and block hashes. They are written
interleaved over 60 transactions, as a growing chain writes them. Each
sub-database is 3 levels deep.

- reads_the_pages_a_cursor_walk_reads: after the data file's pages are
  dropped from the page cache, the warm-up leaves resident exactly the pages
  that a walk through every item with the public cursor API leaves.
  Its counts match the new resident pages.
- reads_the_leaves_only_if_all_of_them_fit_in_the_budget: one byte short of
  the leaf size, it reads only the upper levels, and those pages are a
  subset of the full set. At exactly the leaf size, it reads everything.
- checks_its_arguments: a write transaction, a bad handle, a null stat,
  null handles and a null transaction are refused. An empty table and an
  empty list read nothing. A budget of 0 still reads the upper levels.

The first two need mincore and posix_fadvise. They skip where the page cache
cannot be dropped (a tmpfs), where LMDB pages are not OS pages, and in
MDB_VL32 builds.

Stop function, on the same three-table environment:
- a stop function that never stops changes no count, and is asked once
  per read-ahead request and once per page, no more;
- stopping at the first call requests and reads nothing, and returns 0
  with mw_stopped set;
- stopping among the levels above the leaves, halfway through the
  leaves, and before the last page each stops at that call, with the
  pages before it read and no leaf counted as skipped.

Read-ahead window: stopped halfway through the leaves and given 300 ms for
the requested pages to arrive, the page cache holds at most 64 pages
beyond those read, plus the longest run of adjacent pages (one request
covers a whole run). Against a version of mdb.c without the window it
fails: 2,039 pages ahead, against a bound of 142 (64, plus a run of 78).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
